// Class 9 Social Science practice bank, part 3: Political Science (institutions, rights) and Economics (Palampur to food security).
StudyData.addPractice(9, "sst", {
  "working-of-institutions": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Who is the formal head of the State in India?", options: ["The Prime Minister", "The Chief Justice of India", "The President", "The Speaker of the Lok Sabha"], answer: 2, explain: "The President is the head of the State, with mostly nominal powers. The Prime Minister is the head of the government.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Indian Parliament consists of the Lok Sabha, the Rajya Sabha and the:", options: ["Supreme Court", "Council of Ministers", "Election Commission", "President"], answer: 3, explain: "The President is a part of Parliament but is not a member of either House.", level: 1 },
      { q: "What share of government jobs did the Mandal Commission recommend for the socially and economically backward classes?", options: ["10%", "27%", "33%", "50%"], answer: 1, explain: "The Second Backward Classes Commission recommended 27% reservation of government jobs.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A ministry's Office Memorandum is best described as:", options: ["a communication issued by an officer stating a government decision", "a bill passed by Parliament", "a judgment given by a High Court", "a law made by the President"], answer: 0, explain: "An Office Memorandum is an official communication stating a government decision, such as the Mandal order of 13 August 1990.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The decisions of which court are binding on all other courts in India?", options: ["The District Court", "The High Court of the state", "The Supreme Court", "The Lok Adalat"], answer: 2, explain: "India has an integrated judiciary, and the Supreme Court is at the top; its decisions bind all other courts.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Why do ministers have more power than civil servants in a democracy?", options: ["They have more technical knowledge", "They are elected by the people and represent their will", "They are appointed for life", "They can be removed only by impeachment"], answer: 1, explain: "Ministers are elected for a period and represent the will of the people, which is supreme in a democracy. Civil servants give expert advice and carry out decisions.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The Lok Sabha is more powerful than the Rajya Sabha.\nReason (R): Only the Lok Sabha can pass a no-confidence motion that forces the Council of Ministers to resign.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Control over the Council of Ministers is one of the reasons for the Lok Sabha's greater power, along with its larger numbers in a joint session and its hold over money matters.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The President of India exercises real power in all matters.\nReason (R): All government activities and laws are issued in the name of the President.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Everything is done in the President's name, but the President mostly acts on the advice of the Council of Ministers, so the powers are largely nominal.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "After a Lok Sabha election no party or coalition has a clear majority. Which statement about the President's role is correct?", options: ["The President must appoint the leader of the largest party as PM", "The President uses personal discretion to appoint a person who can command majority support", "The Supreme Court appoints the Prime Minister", "A fresh election is held at once without any appointment"], answer: 1, explain: "When no party or coalition has a clear majority, the President exercises personal discretion in appointing the Prime Minister.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A Prime Minister resigns after losing majority support in the Lok Sabha. What happens to the Council of Ministers?", options: ["Only the Cabinet Ministers resign", "Only the Ministers of State continue", "All ministers continue until the next election", "The entire ministry goes out of office"], answer: 3, explain: "When the PM quits, the entire ministry quits, as the Cabinet works on the principle of collective responsibility.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The Lok Sabha passes a money bill, but the Rajya Sabha strongly disagrees with it. What is the likely outcome?", options: ["The bill is dropped immediately", "The Rajya Sabha can delay it by up to 14 days or suggest changes, which the Lok Sabha may reject", "A joint session decides by secret ballot", "The President returns the bill to the Rajya Sabha for passing"], answer: 1, explain: "The Rajya Sabha cannot reject a money bill. It can only delay it by 14 days or suggest changes that the Lok Sabha is free to ignore.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A motion to remove a Supreme Court judge secures a two-thirds majority in the Lok Sabha but fails in the Rajya Sabha. The judge:", options: ["is removed because the Lok Sabha is more powerful", "is removed after a joint session", "is not removed", "is removed if the Prime Minister agrees"], answer: 2, explain: "Impeachment needs a two-thirds majority in both Houses, passed separately. Failure in one House means the judge stays.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Arrange these steps of the Mandal order in the correct sequence: (1) Supreme Court decision in the Indira Sawhney case, (2) Union Cabinet decision, (3) issue of the Office Memorandum.", options: ["2, 3, 1", "3, 2, 1", "1, 2, 3", "2, 1, 3"], answer: 0, explain: "The Cabinet decided on 6 August 1990, the Office Memorandum was issued on 13 August 1990, and the Supreme Court ruled in 1992.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of these statements about the Prime Minister is correct?\nI. The PM has a fixed tenure of five years.\nII. The PM continues as long as he or she enjoys majority support in the Lok Sabha.\nIII. A no-confidence motion passed by the Lok Sabha forces the PM to resign.", options: ["I and II only", "I and III only", "I, II and III", "II and III only"], answer: 3, explain: "The PM has no fixed tenure and stays only while supported by a Lok Sabha majority. Statement I is false.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the two Houses of the Indian Parliament.", a: "The Lok Sabha (House of the People) and the Rajya Sabha (Council of States).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is judicial review?", a: "It is the power of the courts to examine laws and executive actions and declare them invalid if they violate the Constitution.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the political executive and the permanent executive.", a: "(1) The political executive consists of ministers, who are elected by the people for a specific period and take major policy decisions.\n(2) The permanent executive consists of civil servants appointed on a long-term basis, who stay in office even when the ruling party changes and carry out decisions.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What is a Public Interest Litigation (PIL)? State one benefit.", a: "(1) A PIL is a case that any person or group can file in a court to protect the public interest against a government action or a law, even if they are not personally affected.\n(2) Benefit: it lets the poor and the weak, who cannot approach courts themselves, get justice.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe the composition of the Council of Ministers.", a: "(1) The Council of Ministers usually has 60 to 80 ministers.\n(2) Cabinet Ministers are the top-level leaders in charge of the major ministries, and they meet to take decisions.\n(3) Ministers of State with independent charge head smaller ministries, while Ministers of State assist Cabinet Ministers and attend Cabinet meetings only when invited.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the role of the President of India in relation to the Council of Ministers and to bills passed by Parliament.", a: "(1) The President acts on the advice of the Council of Ministers; she can ask them to reconsider their advice once, but must act on it if it is repeated.\n(2) The President can return a bill once for reconsideration, but must give assent if Parliament passes it again.\n(3) Thus the powers of the President are mostly nominal, and real decisions are taken by the Council of Ministers headed by the PM.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using the Mandal order as an example, show how different institutions work together to take and implement a major decision.", a: "(1) The Union Cabinet, headed by the PM, took the decision to implement the Mandal recommendations, and the PM informed Parliament.\n(2) Civil servants of the Department of Personnel and Training drafted and issued the Office Memorandum (13 August 1990), thereby implementing the decision.\n(3) When the order was challenged, the Supreme Court in the Indira Sawhney case (1992) upheld it but asked for exclusion of well-to-do persons among the backward classes.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the powers of Parliament in India.", a: "(1) Law making: Parliament is the final authority for making laws; in a deadlock a joint session is held.\n(2) Control over the executive: ministers are responsible to the Lok Sabha, and the government must resign if it loses its majority through a no-confidence motion.\n(3) Control over money: the government can spend only what Parliament sanctions; money bills need the Lok Sabha's approval.\n(4) Debate: it is the highest forum of discussion on public issues, policies and national matters.\n(5) It also has other powers, such as the impeachment of judges and discussion of the budget, while the Rajya Sabha especially looks after the interests of the states.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Describe the structure of the Indian judiciary. How is its independence ensured and what is its power of judicial review?", a: "(1) Structure: India has an integrated judiciary, with the Supreme Court at the top, High Courts in the states, and District and local courts below.\n(2) The decisions of the Supreme Court are binding on all other courts, and it is the highest court of appeal.\n(3) Independence: the judiciary is not under the control of the legislature or the executive. Judges are appointed by the President on the advice of the PM and in consultation with the Chief Justice, and in practice senior judges select new judges.\n(4) A judge can be removed only by impeachment, passed by a two-thirds majority in both Houses separately, which is very difficult.\n(5) Judicial review: the courts can strike down any law or executive action that violates the Constitution, and through PIL anyone can approach the court in public interest.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Parliament passes a bill with a majority in the Lok Sabha. The President feels the bill needs more thought and sends it back. Parliament passes the same bill again without changes. Some citizens believe that one provision of the new law takes away a Fundamental Right.\n(i) What can the President do the first time, and what must she do after Parliament passes it again?\n(ii) Which institution can the citizens approach?\n(iii) What power of that institution can be used against the provision?", a: "(i) The President can return the bill once for reconsideration; when Parliament passes it again, she has to give her assent.\n(ii) The citizens can approach the courts, that is, the High Court or the Supreme Court, and can also file a PIL.\n(iii) The power of judicial review: the court can declare the provision invalid if it violates the Constitution.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "democratic-rights": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The practice of forcing workers to give service free or for a nominal payment is called:", options: ["lockout", "begar", "strike", "boycott"], answer: 1, explain: "Begar is forced labour, which is banned under the Right against Exploitation.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In Kosovo, the victims of the massacre by Serb forces were:", options: ["Muslims of Saudi Arabia", "ethnic Albanians", "American soldiers", "Serb civilians"], answer: 1, explain: "Serb forces under Milosevic carried out a massacre of ethnic Albanians in Kosovo.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Formal orders of the Supreme Court or High Courts to enforce Fundamental Rights are called:", options: ["ordinances", "writs", "bills", "memorandums"], answer: 1, explain: "Courts issue directions, orders or writs to enforce Fundamental Rights.", level: 1 },
      { q: "How many Fundamental Rights are given in the Indian Constitution as covered in the chapter?", options: ["Four", "Five", "Seven", "Six"], answer: 3, explain: "The six Fundamental Rights are Equality, Freedom, against Exploitation, Freedom of Religion, Cultural and Educational Rights, and Constitutional Remedies.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A right is a reasonable claim that is recognised by society and:", options: ["decided by the majority", "sanctioned by law", "given by the ruling party", "approved by the army"], answer: 1, explain: "A right must be recognised by society and sanctioned by law.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which statement about reservations for SC, ST and OBC is correct?", options: ["They violate the Right to Equality", "They are against equal opportunity in public employment", "They are consistent with the Right to Equality", "They apply only in private companies"], answer: 2, explain: "Such reservations are not a violation of the Right to Equality.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A government school makes the prayers of one religion compulsory for every student. Which right is violated?", options: ["Right to Equality", "Right to Freedom of Religion", "Right against Exploitation", "Right to Information"], answer: 1, explain: "India is secular; no religious instruction is given in government institutions and nobody can be forced to follow a religion.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The freedoms under the Right to Freedom are absolute.\nReason (R): Citizens enjoy these freedoms subject to reasonable restrictions.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "The freedoms are subject to reasonable restrictions in the interest of public order and security, so they are not absolute.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A man is arrested and kept in a police station for three days without being produced before a magistrate. Which right is violated?", options: ["Right to life and personal liberty", "Cultural and Educational Rights", "Right to Equality in public places", "Right to Information"], answer: 0, explain: "An arrested person must be produced before the nearest magistrate within 24 hours.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A linguistic minority community wants to open a school to preserve its own language and script. Which right allows this?", options: ["Right against Exploitation", "Right to Equality", "Right to Constitutional Remedies", "Cultural and Educational Rights"], answer: 3, explain: "Minorities can conserve their language and culture and set up educational institutions of their choice.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of the following is NOT a protection given to an arrested person?", options: ["To be told the reasons for the arrest", "To be produced before the nearest magistrate within 24 hours", "To consult a lawyer", "To be kept in custody for a week without a magistrate's permission"], answer: 3, explain: "Custody beyond 24 hours needs a magistrate's order. The other three are protections of an arrested person.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The National Human Rights Commission is not a court.\nReason (R): It investigates complaints of rights violations and recommends action but does not decide court cases.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The NHRC is an independent body set up by law in 1993; it inquires and recommends and does not give court judgments.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Which pair of right and example is correctly matched?", options: ["Right against Exploitation: a ban on children below 14 in hazardous work", "Right to Equality: right to move freely across the country", "Right to Freedom: abolition of untouchability", "Right to Freedom of Religion: right to approach the Supreme Court"], answer: 0, explain: "Employing children below 14 in factories, mines and hazardous work is banned. Free movement is under Freedom, abolition of untouchability is under Equality, and approaching the courts is under Constitutional Remedies.", level: 3 },
      { q: "What is the best conclusion from the Kosovo example?", options: ["Only monarchies violate rights", "Even an elected government can violate the rights of a group of people", "Rights are needed only in poor countries", "Minorities have no need of rights in democracies"], answer: 1, explain: "Kosovo showed that an elected leader can still commit atrocities, so rights must protect minorities even in a democracy.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What are Fundamental Rights?", a: "Fundamental Rights are the basic rights guaranteed by the Constitution which are essential for life and which even the government cannot violate.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Which Act of 2005 allows citizens to seek information from government offices?", a: "The Right to Information (RTI) Act, 2005.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why are the Saudi Arabian people said to lack democratic rights?", a: "A hereditary king rules and the people have no role in choosing or changing rulers; they cannot form political parties, and women face many public restrictions.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State any two provisions of the Right against Exploitation.", a: "(1) It bans traffic in human beings and forced labour (begar).\n(2) It prohibits the employment of children below 14 years in factories, mines or hazardous work.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why is India called a secular state? Mention two features of the Right to Freedom of Religion.", a: "(1) India is secular because the state has no official religion and treats all religions equally.\n(2) Every person can profess, practise and propagate a religion but cannot force others to convert; no religious instruction is given in government educational institutions.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "How does the Right to Equality protect citizens? Give two points.", a: "(1) All are equal before the law, and there can be no discrimination on grounds of religion, caste, sex or place of birth in access to shops, hotels, wells and roads.\n(2) There is equal opportunity in government jobs and untouchability is abolished.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain the Right to Equality in the Indian Constitution.", a: "(1) Equality before law: everyone, including the highest officials, is subject to the same laws.\n(2) No discrimination: the state cannot discriminate on grounds of religion, caste, ethnicity, sex or place of birth, and nobody can be denied access to shops, hotels, wells, roads and similar places.\n(3) Equal opportunity in government jobs, and untouchability is abolished. Reservations for SC, ST and OBC do not violate this right.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the Cultural and Educational Rights.", a: "(1) Any section of citizens with a distinct language or culture has the right to conserve it.\n(2) All minorities can set up and run educational institutions of their choice.\n(3) Admission to an institution maintained or aided by the government cannot be denied on the ground of religion or language.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What did the experience of Guantanamo Bay show about the need for rights? Answer in three points.", a: "(1) About 600 people were secretly picked up and held for years without trial, and their families were not informed.\n(2) Amnesty International reported that they were being tortured, which shows what happens in the absence of legal protection.\n(3) It shows that rights are needed to protect people from arbitrary action by governments, even powerful ones.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how the Right to Constitutional Remedies and other institutions help in protecting the Fundamental Rights.", a: "(1) Under the Right to Constitutional Remedies, a citizen can go directly to the Supreme Court or a High Court if a Fundamental Right is violated; Dr Ambedkar called it the heart and soul of the Constitution.\n(2) Courts can issue writs, orders and directions to enforce the rights.\n(3) Courts can strike down laws and actions that violate Fundamental Rights.\n(4) Through PIL, any person or group can approach the court on behalf of others.\n(5) The NHRC, set up by law in 1993, investigates complaints and recommends action, although it cannot decide cases.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "How has the scope of rights expanded in India? Give examples.", a: "(1) Rights are not limited to the six Fundamental Rights; their scope keeps expanding through courts and laws.\n(2) The Right to Information Act (2005) lets citizens seek information from government offices.\n(3) The Right to Education makes free and compulsory education of children up to 14 years a duty of the government.\n(4) Courts have expanded the right to life to include the right to food.\n(5) Some constitutions, like that of South Africa, also give rights to privacy, housing, health care, food and water, and international covenants recognise rights such as the right to work and to health.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Ramesh, a labourer, is arrested by the police on suspicion. He is not told why he is held and is not allowed to meet a lawyer. After two days, his family approaches the High Court.\n(i) Which right of Ramesh has been violated?\n(ii) What should the police have done within 24 hours?\n(iii) Which Fundamental Right allows his family to approach the High Court?", a: "(i) The right to life and personal liberty, which is part of the Right to Freedom.\n(ii) They should have told him the reasons for arrest, allowed him to consult a lawyer and produced him before the nearest magistrate within 24 hours.\n(iii) The Right to Constitutional Remedies.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "village-palampur": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "About what share of the working people in Palampur depend on farming?", options: ["25%", "50%", "75%", "90%"], answer: 2, explain: "Farming is the main activity, supporting about 75% of working people; the other 25% are in non-farm activities.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The knowledge and enterprise needed to put together land, labour and physical capital to produce output is called:", options: ["working capital", "fixed capital", "natural capital", "human capital"], answer: 3, explain: "Human capital is the knowledge and enterprise that combines the other factors of production.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Growing more than one crop on the same piece of land during a year is called:", options: ["multiple cropping", "crop rotation", "fallowing", "shifting cultivation"], answer: 0, explain: "Multiple cropping is the most common way of increasing production on a given piece of land.", level: 1 },
      { q: "With HYV seeds the wheat yield in Palampur rose to about:", options: ["1,300 kg per hectare", "3,200 kg per hectare", "5,000 kg per hectare", "800 kg per hectare"], answer: 1, explain: "Wheat yield rose from about 1,300 kg per hectare with traditional seeds to about 3,200 kg per hectare with HYV seeds.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is an example of fixed capital?", options: ["Seeds", "Chemical fertiliser", "A tubewell", "Money in hand"], answer: 2, explain: "Tools, machines and buildings such as a tractor or tubewell are used over many years. Seeds, fertiliser and cash are used up in production.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which development made multiple cropping possible in Palampur?", options: ["Expansion of the cultivated area after 1960", "Fall in the price of land", "Use of the Persian wheel on all fields", "A well-developed system of irrigation with electric tubewells"], answer: 3, explain: "Electric tubewells replaced the Persian wheel and gave assured irrigation. The cultivated area has not expanded since 1960.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Modern farming methods can threaten the sustainability of farming.\nReason (R): Overuse of chemical fertilisers reduces soil fertility, and overuse of tubewells lowers the groundwater level.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Soil fertility and groundwater are natural resources that are hard to restore once destroyed, which is why overuse harms sustainability.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Land is a fixed factor of production in Palampur.\nReason (R): Multiple cropping is a common way of raising total production from the same land.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both are true: the cultivated area has not grown since 1960. But multiple cropping does not explain why land is fixed; it is a response to the fixed land.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Meena grows wheat on 2 hectares. If she changes from traditional seeds (1,300 kg per hectare) to HYV seeds (3,200 kg per hectare), her wheat output rises by:", options: ["1,900 kg", "3,800 kg", "2,600 kg", "6,400 kg"], answer: 1, explain: "Increase per hectare = 3,200 − 1,300 = 1,900 kg. For 2 hectares: 1,900 × 2 = 3,800 kg.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Rahim buys goods from a wholesale market in a nearby town and sells them in his village shop. His work is an example of:", options: ["farming", "a non-farm activity", "a primary sector activity", "multiple cropping"], answer: 1, explain: "Shopkeeping and trade are non-farm activities that employ about a quarter of Palampur's working people.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The wheat yield in Palampur rose from 1,300 to 3,200 kg per hectare. The percentage increase is about:", options: ["59%", "146%", "190%", "246%"], answer: 1, explain: "Increase = 1,900 kg. Percentage increase = 1,900 ÷ 1,300 × 100 ≈ 146%. The 59% option wrongly divides by 3,200.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A rectangular field measures 200 m × 150 m. Its area is:", options: ["0.3 hectare", "30 hectares", "3 hectares", "300 hectares"], answer: 2, explain: "Area = 200 × 150 = 30,000 m². One hectare is 10,000 m², so the area is 3 hectares.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Palampur has about 450 families, of which 60 cultivate more than 2 hectares. What percentage of families is this, roughly?", options: ["About 13%", "About 27%", "About 33%", "About 53%"], answer: 0, explain: "60 ÷ 450 × 100 ≈ 13.3%. The one-third (33%) figure is for the 150 landless families.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of these statements about Palampur are correct?\nI. The Green Revolution came in the late 1960s.\nII. HYV seeds need plenty of water, fertilisers and pesticides.\nIII. The cultivated area has expanded steadily since 1960.", options: ["I, II and III", "II and III only", "I and III only", "I and II only"], answer: 3, explain: "Statements I and II are correct. The cultivated area has not expanded since 1960, so III is false.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a hectare?", a: "A hectare is the area of a square with sides of 100 m, that is, 10,000 m².", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is meant by the yield of a crop?", a: "Yield is the crop produced on a given piece of land during a single season, for example, kg per hectare.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name two non-farm activities carried out in Palampur.", a: "Any two: dairy, small-scale manufacturing (such as making jaggery), shopkeeping, transport, running a computer class.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between fixed capital and working capital with one example each.", a: "(1) Fixed capital consists of tools, machines and buildings that are used in production over many years, e.g., a tractor or tubewell.\n(2) Working capital consists of raw materials and money in hand that are used up in production, e.g., seeds and fertilisers.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why is the use of modern farming methods a threat to sustainability? Give two reasons.", a: "(1) Overuse of chemical fertilisers reduces soil fertility.\n(2) Overuse of tubewells has lowered the groundwater level; both resources are difficult to restore once destroyed.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why do most small farmers of Palampur borrow money, and from whom?", a: "(1) They have no savings and need working capital for seeds, fertilisers and other inputs.\n(2) They borrow from large farmers, moneylenders or traders at high interest rates, and so fall into debt.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Compare the incomes and working conditions of farm labourers with those of medium and large farmers in Palampur.", a: "(1) Farm labourers belong to landless or small-farmer families and work for wages in cash or in kind.\n(2) Because many labourers compete for few jobs, they often get less than the minimum wage set by the government.\n(3) Medium and large farmers have a surplus produce which they sell, so they earn more and can invest in capital such as tractors.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How did the Green Revolution change farming in Palampur? Answer in three points.", a: "(1) Farmers started using HYV seeds, chemical fertilisers, pesticides and assured irrigation.\n(2) Wheat yield increased from about 1,300 kg per hectare to about 3,200 kg per hectare.\n(3) It required more capital for inputs, and overuse of fertilisers and groundwater began to harm the soil and water.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe three non-farm activities in Palampur.", a: "(1) Dairy: many families keep buffaloes, and milk is sold at Raiganj where chilling centres have opened.\n(2) Small-scale manufacturing, such as jaggery making, done at home or in fields with family labour.\n(3) Shopkeeping and transport: shopkeepers sell goods bought from wholesale markets in the city, and rickshaw, tractor and truck drivers provide transport.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe how land is distributed among the families of Palampur. How does this inequality affect the poor?", a: "(1) Out of about 450 families, nearly 150 are landless, mostly dalits.\n(2) Around 240 families cultivate small plots of less than 2 hectares.\n(3) Only about 60 medium and large farmers cultivate more than 2 hectares.\n(4) Small plots cannot give enough income, so small farmers also work as labourers.\n(5) Landless people depend on wages, often below the minimum wage, and the poor borrow at high interest.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Explain the ways of increasing production from a given piece of land. Mention the drawbacks of modern farming.", a: "(1) Land is fixed, so production has to be raised from the same land.\n(2) Multiple cropping: growing more than one crop in a year, which needs good irrigation such as electric tubewells.\n(3) Modern farming methods: HYV seeds, chemical fertilisers, pesticides and irrigation raise the yield per hectare, as in the Green Revolution.\n(4) These need more capital to buy inputs.\n(5) Drawbacks: overuse of chemical fertilisers reduces soil fertility and overuse of tubewells lowers the groundwater, so production may not be sustainable.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Suman lives in a village near a small town. She uses an electric motor to run a small flour mill in her house, with the help of her family. She buys wheat from nearby farmers and sells flour in the village.\n(i) Which type of activity is this: farm or non-farm?\n(ii) Name one item of fixed capital and one of working capital in her work.\n(iii) Mention one thing that would help such activities grow.", a: "(i) A non-farm activity (small-scale manufacturing).\n(ii) Fixed capital: the flour mill or electric motor. Working capital: the wheat she buys (or money in hand).\n(iii) Loans at low interest rates, and good roads, transport and telephone links to markets in towns and cities.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "people-as-resource": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "'People as resource' refers to a country's working people in terms of their:", options: ["total number only", "productive skills and abilities", "land holdings", "religious beliefs"], answer: 1, explain: "The term describes the working people of a country in terms of their existing productive skills and abilities.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Mining and fishing belong to which sector?", options: ["Secondary", "Tertiary", "Quaternary", "Primary"], answer: 3, explain: "Agriculture, forestry, fishing, animal husbandry and mining are primary sector activities.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In India the labour force includes people in the age group of:", options: ["15 to 59 years", "18 to 65 years", "6 to 14 years", "21 to 60 years"], answer: 0, explain: "The labour force includes people aged 15 to 59 years.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan aims to provide elementary education to children aged:", options: ["3 to 6 years", "6 to 14 years", "14 to 18 years", "5 to 10 years"], answer: 1, explain: "It aimed to give elementary education to all children aged 6–14 years.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The infant mortality rate refers to the death of children below the age of:", options: ["five years", "six months", "one month", "one year"], answer: 3, explain: "IMR is the death of a child under one year of age, counted per 1,000 live births.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A farmer who grows vegetables and eats them at home is doing a:", options: ["non-market activity", "market activity", "tertiary activity", "secondary activity"], answer: 0, explain: "Production for self-consumption is a non-market activity; market activities are done for pay or profit.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Japan has become a developed country despite having few natural resources.\nReason (R): Japan has invested in its people, especially in education and health.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Japan's investment in human resources turned its people into a productive asset.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Disguised unemployment is clearly visible because workers have no job at all.\nReason (R): In disguised unemployment, more people work on a task than are actually needed.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Disguised unemployment is hidden because everyone appears to be working, even though some add nothing to output.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Eight members of a family work on a small farm that needs only five workers. This is a case of:", options: ["seasonal unemployment", "educated unemployment", "disguised unemployment", "full employment"], answer: 2, explain: "Three extra workers add nothing to output, so their unemployment is hidden.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Anita has a postgraduate degree but cannot find a job in the city although she is willing to work at the going wage. This is:", options: ["disguised unemployment", "educated unemployment", "seasonal unemployment", "voluntary leisure"], answer: 1, explain: "Educated unemployment is common in urban areas, where many degree-holders cannot find work.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which list correctly matches the sector with the activity: fisherman, factory worker, bank clerk?", options: ["Secondary, primary, tertiary", "Primary, secondary, tertiary", "Tertiary, secondary, primary", "Primary, tertiary, secondary"], answer: 1, explain: "Fishing is primary, manufacturing is secondary and banking is a tertiary service.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Sakal finished his education and a computer course and got a good job, while Vilas could not study and remained a poor fish seller. The best conclusion is that:", options: ["luck alone decides income", "physical capital is more important than people", "investment in education and skills raises a person's earning capacity", "fish sellers cannot earn at all"], answer: 2, explain: "Education and training build human capital, which raises productivity and income.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of the following is NOT an effect of unemployment?", options: ["Wastage of manpower", "Hopelessness among the youth", "More dependence on earning members", "An improved quality of life for the family"], answer: 3, explain: "Unemployment lowers the quality of life of the family and the community.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of these statements is correct?\nI. Women's household work is not paid and is not counted in national income.\nII. Education and health raise the productivity of workers.\nIII. All unemployment in India is seasonal.", options: ["I and III only", "I, II and III", "II and III only", "I and II only"], answer: 3, explain: "I and II are correct. India has seasonal, disguised and educated unemployment, so III is false.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is human capital formation?", a: "It is the development of the human resource through investment in education, training and health so that people become more productive.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is meant by seasonal unemployment?", a: "It is the situation in which people cannot find work during some months of the year, such as farm workers between sowing and harvesting.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give one example of an economic activity in each of the three sectors.", a: "Primary: farming (or fishing). Secondary: manufacturing in a factory. Tertiary: banking (or teaching, transport).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between market activities and non-market activities.", a: "(1) Market activities are performed for pay or profit, that is, production for exchange, e.g., a tailor sewing clothes for customers.\n(2) Non-market activities are production for self-consumption, e.g., a farmer keeping part of the crop for his own family.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why is women's household work not counted in the national income? Give two reasons.", a: "(1) Women's household work is not paid, so it has no market value.\n(2) Only market activities for pay or profit are counted; yet when women work outside they often earn less because of lower education and skills.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "How does health contribute to the quality of a country's population? Give two points.", a: "(1) A healthy person can work more efficiently and so contributes more to the growth of the country.\n(2) The fall in infant mortality and the rise in life expectancy show better health of the population.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain how investment in education and health benefits an individual and the country.", a: "(1) Educated and healthy people earn higher incomes and become more productive.\n(2) Education opens new aspirations and develops values of life, and healthy workers work more efficiently.\n(3) The country gains because human capital adds to productive power, as in Japan, which developed by investing in people despite few natural resources.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the three types of unemployment found in India with one example of each.", a: "(1) Seasonal unemployment: people lack work for some months, e.g., farm workers between sowing and harvesting.\n(2) Disguised unemployment: more people work than needed, e.g., eight members working on a farm that needs five.\n(3) Educated unemployment: degree-holders in urban areas cannot find suitable jobs.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "State three effects of unemployment on the economy and society.", a: "(1) Wastage of manpower, and people who are an asset become a liability.\n(2) Hopelessness and despair among the youth, and more dependence on the earning members.\n(3) A fall in the quality of life of the family and community.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the three sectors of economic activity with examples. How are market and non-market activities different?", a: "(1) The primary sector includes agriculture, forestry, animal husbandry, fishing, poultry farming, mining and quarrying.\n(2) The secondary sector is manufacturing, e.g., making cloth in a factory.\n(3) The tertiary sector includes trade, transport, communication, banking, education, health, tourism and insurance.\n(4) Market activities are done for pay or profit and are counted in national income.\n(5) Non-market activities are production for self-consumption, such as household work, which is unpaid and not counted.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Discuss how education, health and unemployment affect the quality of the population and the economy of India.", a: "(1) Quality of population depends on the literacy rate, health and skill formation, and it decides a country's growth rate.\n(2) Education gives new aspirations and values; the Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan aimed at elementary education for children aged 6–14, and the mid-day meal scheme improves attendance and nutrition.\n(3) Health makes people efficient; a fall in IMR and a rise in life expectancy show improvement.\n(4) Unemployment in India takes the forms of seasonal, disguised and educated unemployment.\n(5) It wastes manpower, creates despair and dependence, and lowers the quality of life.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: In a village, nine members of a family work on a field that really needs only five workers. The other four add nothing to the output. Their cousin Jyoti has a postgraduate degree but has been unable to find work in the city for over a year, though she is ready to work.\n(i) Name the type of unemployment on the family's field.\n(ii) Name the type of unemployment Jyoti faces.\n(iii) State one effect of such unemployment on the family.", a: "(i) Disguised unemployment.\n(ii) Educated unemployment, which is common in urban areas.\n(iii) The family depends more on the earning members, and the quality of life of the family falls; the youth may feel hopeless.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "poverty-as-a-challenge": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The calorie norm used for the poverty line in rural areas is:", options: ["1,800 calories per person per day", "2,100 calories per person per day", "2,400 calories per person per day", "2,700 calories per person per day"], answer: 2, explain: "The norm is 2,400 calories per person per day in rural areas and 2,100 in urban areas.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Poverty estimates in India are prepared through sample surveys conducted by the:", options: ["National Sample Survey Organisation", "Election Commission", "Reserve Bank of India", "Food Corporation of India"], answer: 0, explain: "The NSSO conducts periodic sample surveys to estimate poverty.", level: 1 },
      { q: "MGNREGA aims to provide wage employment to every rural household for:", options: ["50 days a year", "100 days a year", "150 days a year", "200 days a year"], answer: 1, explain: "MGNREGA (2005) aims at 100 days of wage employment per rural household each year, with one-third of the jobs reserved for women.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which two social groups are the most vulnerable to poverty in India?", options: ["Brahmins and Kshatriyas", "OBC and general category households", "Urban and rural middle classes", "Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes"], answer: 3, explain: "SC and ST households are the most vulnerable social groups. Rural agricultural labour and urban casual labour are the most vulnerable economic groups.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The proportion of people below the poverty line in a population is known as the:", options: ["Head Count Ratio", "Poverty gap", "Literacy rate", "Dependency ratio"], answer: 0, explain: "The Head Count Ratio measures the share of poor people in the total population.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which state reduced poverty mainly through land reforms?", options: ["Kerala", "West Bengal", "Punjab", "Tamil Nadu"], answer: 1, explain: "West Bengal used land reforms; Kerala focused on human resource development, Punjab and Haryana on agricultural growth, and Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu on public distribution of food grains.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The calorie norm of the poverty line is higher in rural areas than in urban areas.\nReason (R): People in rural areas do more physical work and need more energy.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The rural norm is 2,400 calories against 2,100 in urban areas, because rural work is more physical.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Income is the only measure of poverty.\nReason (R): Social exclusion and vulnerability are also seen as important aspects of poverty.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Poverty also includes social exclusion, vulnerability, lack of shelter, clean water and education, so income alone is not a full measure.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Mohan is a daily-wage labourer. A flood destroys his workplace and he loses his job, so his family is now likely to remain poor for the coming years. This illustrates:", options: ["vulnerability", "the Head Count Ratio", "disguised unemployment", "a calorie norm"], answer: 0, explain: "Vulnerability is the greater probability of becoming or remaining poor because of events like disasters or job loss.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A group of poor women in a village save together, take a bank loan with a government subsidy and start a small business. This follows the idea of which scheme?", options: ["PMRY", "SGSY", "PMGY", "REGP"], answer: 1, explain: "The Swarnajayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana (1999) organises poor families into self-help groups with bank credit and subsidy.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A rural family of 5 spends ₹4,000 a month. Compared with the 2011–12 rural poverty line of ₹816 per person per month, the family is:", options: ["above the poverty line, as ₹4,000 is greater than ₹816", "below the poverty line, as ₹800 per person is less than ₹816", "exactly on the poverty line", "above the line, since the urban line is ₹1,000"], answer: 1, explain: "Per person expenditure = 4,000 ÷ 5 = ₹800, which is below ₹816. The poverty line is per person, not per family.", level: 3 },
      { q: "An urban family of 4 spends ₹4,200 a month. Using the 2011–12 urban poverty line of ₹1,000 per person, the family is:", options: ["below the line, as ₹4,200 is less than ₹4,816", "below the line, as 4,200 ÷ 4 is less than ₹816", "above the line, as ₹1,050 per person is more than ₹1,000", "on the line"], answer: 2, explain: "4,200 ÷ 4 = ₹1,050 per person, which is more than the urban line of ₹1,000.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which pair of state and measure is incorrectly matched?", options: ["Kerala: human resource development", "Punjab and Haryana: high agricultural growth", "West Bengal: land reforms", "Tamil Nadu: land reforms and high agricultural growth"], answer: 3, explain: "Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu are credited with the public distribution of food grains; Tamil Nadu is not matched with land reforms.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of these statements is correct?\nI. The poverty line is the same in all countries.\nII. MGNREGA reserves one-third of the proposed jobs for women.\nIII. SC and ST households are the most vulnerable social groups.", options: ["I and II only", "I and III only", "II and III only", "I, II and III"], answer: 2, explain: "The poverty line differs between countries and over time, so I is false.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is meant by the poverty line?", a: "It is the minimum level of income or consumption needed to fulfil basic needs; people below it are classified as poor.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is social exclusion?", a: "It is a process through which the poor are kept out of facilities, benefits and opportunities that better-off people enjoy.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the programme started in 2005 to give rural households 100 days of wage employment.", a: "The Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA), 2005.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State two features of MGNREGA.", a: "(1) It aims to provide 100 days of wage employment every year to every rural household.\n(2) One-third of the proposed jobs are reserved for women.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Give two reasons why poor people in India remain in debt.", a: "(1) Small farmers and the poor borrow to meet needs and cannot repay because of low incomes.\n(2) Poor families spend heavily on social obligations such as marriages and religious ceremonies, which leads to indebtedness.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain how the states of Kerala and West Bengal reduced poverty.", a: "(1) Kerala reduced poverty by focusing on human resource development.\n(2) West Bengal reduced poverty through land reforms.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "How is the poverty line determined in India? Why do the calorie norms differ between rural and urban areas?", a: "(1) It is based on the money needed for minimum food (calories), clothing, footwear, fuel and light, education and medical needs.\n(2) The calorie norm is 2,400 calories per person per day in rural areas and 2,100 in urban areas.\n(3) The rural norm is higher because rural people do more physical work.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain inequality within families as an aspect of poverty.", a: "(1) Poverty is not shared equally within a household.\n(2) Women, elderly people and female infants are often denied equal access to the family's resources.\n(3) This makes women, the old and girls the poorest among the poor.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the term 'vulnerability' to poverty. Which groups are most vulnerable?", a: "(1) Vulnerability is the greater probability of certain communities or individuals becoming or remaining poor in the coming years, for example, during disasters or job loss.\n(2) Socially, the most vulnerable are SC and ST households.\n(3) Economically, rural agricultural labour households and urban casual labour households are the most vulnerable.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the main causes of poverty in India.", a: "(1) Low economic development under British rule, which ruined traditional handicrafts and discouraged industries such as textiles.\n(2) High growth rate of population along with slow economic growth kept per capita income low.\n(3) Unequal distribution of land, since land reforms were not properly implemented.\n(4) Lack of jobs, as the Green Revolution helped only some regions and industry did not create enough jobs, with huge income inequalities.\n(5) Social and cultural factors, such as heavy spending on social obligations, lead to indebtedness.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Describe the anti-poverty measures taken by the Government of India.", a: "(1) The government follows a two-part strategy: promoting economic growth and targeted anti-poverty programmes.\n(2) The Prime Minister Rozgar Yojana (1993) and the Rural Employment Generation Programme (1995) create self-employment opportunities in rural areas and small towns.\n(3) The Swarnajayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana (1999) organises the poor into self-help groups with bank credit and subsidy.\n(4) The Pradhan Mantri Gramodaya Yojana (2000) provides additional central assistance for primary health, primary education, rural shelter, drinking water and electrification.\n(5) MGNREGA (2005) aims at 100 days of wage employment for every rural household.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: For 2011–12 the poverty line was ₹816 per person per month in rural areas and ₹1,000 in urban areas. A rural family of 4 members spends ₹3,500 a month, while an urban family of 3 spends ₹3,300 a month.\n(i) Find the per-person monthly expenditure of each family.\n(ii) Which family is below the poverty line?\n(iii) Why is the urban poverty line given at a different level?", a: "(i) Rural: 3,500 ÷ 4 = ₹875. Urban: 3,300 ÷ 3 = ₹1,100.\n(ii) Neither is below its line: ₹875 is above ₹816 and ₹1,100 is above ₹1,000.\n(iii) The poverty line is based on the money needed for basic needs, and the calorie norm and cost of living differ between rural and urban areas.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "food-security-india": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The Bengal Famine, which killed about thirty lakh people, occurred in:", options: ["1918", "1943", "1965", "1975"], answer: 1, explain: "The Bengal Famine of 1943 was the most devastating famine in India.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which agency procures food grains from farmers for the buffer stock?", options: ["Reserve Bank of India", "NABARD", "Food Corporation of India", "Mother Dairy"], answer: 2, explain: "The FCI buys wheat and rice from surplus states and stores them as buffer stock.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Minimum Support Price is announced by the government:", options: ["before the sowing season", "after the harvest", "at the time of sale in ration shops", "every five years"], answer: 0, explain: "The MSP is declared every year before the sowing season to encourage farmers to produce more.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Having enough money to buy sufficient, safe and nutritious food is called:", options: ["availability", "accessibility", "storage", "affordability"], answer: 3, explain: "Affordability means an individual has enough money; availability is the supply of food, and accessibility means food is within reach.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which ration card is meant for the poorest of the poor?", options: ["Antyodaya card", "APL card", "BPL card", "Annapurna card"], answer: 0, explain: "Antyodaya cards are for the poorest of the poor, BPL cards for those below the poverty line and APL cards for all others.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Hunger that is related to the cycles of food growing and harvesting is called:", options: ["chronic hunger", "seasonal hunger", "hidden hunger", "permanent hunger"], answer: 1, explain: "Seasonal hunger affects seasonal farm workers and casual labourers, who have no work for some months.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Food security depends only on the availability of food grains in the country.\nReason (R): Accessibility and affordability of food are also parts of food security.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Food security means availability, accessibility and affordability of food for all at all times.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The issue price of food grains in ration shops is lower than the market price.\nReason (R): The government wants the poor to be able to buy food grains at affordable rates.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The issue price is set below the market price so that the poor can afford grains.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A drought in a region lowers food production and pushes up prices, so poor families can no longer buy enough food. Which dimension of food security is most directly affected?", options: ["Availability of stocks in godowns", "Affordability for the poor", "Self-sufficiency in wheat", "Procurement by the FCI"], answer: 1, explain: "Higher prices put food out of the reach of the poor even if some food exists, so affordability suffers.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A ration shop owner sells part of the subsidised rice in the open market and gives poor-quality grain to card holders. Which problem of the PDS does this show?", options: ["Overflowing FCI granaries", "Rising MSP", "Diversion of grains and sale of poor-quality grain by dealers", "Shortage of ration cards"], answer: 2, explain: "Diversion of grain to the open market and poor-quality grain are known problems of ration shops.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Under the Antyodaya Anna Yojana, a poorest family gets 35 kg of grain: 20 kg of wheat at ₹2 per kg and 15 kg of rice at ₹3 per kg. The total amount paid is:", options: ["₹70", "₹105", "₹175", "₹85"], answer: 3, explain: "Wheat: 20 × 2 = ₹40. Rice: 15 × 3 = ₹45. Total = ₹85.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Arrange the following schemes in the order of their introduction: (1) Revamped PDS, (2) Food-for-Work, (3) Antyodaya Anna Yojana, (4) National Food Security Act.", options: ["2, 1, 3, 4", "1, 2, 3, 4", "2, 3, 1, 4", "1, 3, 2, 4"], answer: 0, explain: "Food-for-Work (1977–78), Revamped PDS (1992), AAY (2000) and the National Food Security Act (2013).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which pair is incorrectly matched?", options: ["Amul: White Revolution", "Academy of Development Science: grain banks", "Tamil Nadu: a large share of fair price shops run by cooperatives", "Mother Dairy: grain banks in Maharashtra"], answer: 3, explain: "Mother Dairy sells milk and vegetables at controlled rates in Delhi. Grain banks were set up with the help of the Academy of Development Science in Maharashtra.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which statements about the National Food Security Act, 2013 are correct?\nI. It provides food and nutritional security at affordable prices.\nII. It covers 75% of the rural and 50% of the urban population as eligible households.\nIII. It was introduced soon after the Bengal Famine.", options: ["I and II only", "II and III only", "I and III only", "I, II and III"], answer: 0, explain: "The Act came in 2013. The Bengal Famine was in 1943, so III is false.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a buffer stock?", a: "It is the stock of food grains, mainly wheat and rice, procured by the government through the FCI to meet shortages and distribute to the poor.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the Minimum Support Price (MSP)?", a: "It is a pre-announced price at which the government buys food grains from farmers, declared before the sowing season.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the three dimensions of food security.", a: "Availability, accessibility and affordability.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between chronic hunger and seasonal hunger.", a: "(1) Chronic hunger results from diets that are persistently inadequate in quantity or quality because of very low income.\n(2) Seasonal hunger is related to the cycles of food growing and harvesting and affects seasonal workers who have no work for some months.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State two problems of the Public Distribution System.", a: "(1) FCI godowns overflow with grain that may rot, and a rising MSP increases the cost of maintaining stocks.\n(2) Some ration shop dealers divert grains to the open market or sell poor-quality grains.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why does the government announce the MSP before the sowing season?", a: "(1) Farmers get an assured price before sowing, so they are encouraged to grow more.\n(2) It helps the government procure enough grain for the buffer stock.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Who are the food-insecure people in India? Answer in three points.", a: "(1) In rural areas: landless people, traditional artisans, petty self-employed workers and destitutes.\n(2) In urban areas: people in ill-paid occupations and casual labour.\n(3) Socially, SCs, STs and some OBCs, and people affected by natural disasters; pregnant and nursing mothers and children below five are most affected by malnutrition.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how the buffer stock and the PDS work together.", a: "(1) The FCI buys wheat and rice from farmers in surplus states at the MSP and stores them as buffer stock.\n(2) The grains are sent to deficit areas and distributed through ration shops using ration cards.\n(3) They are sold at the issue price, which is lower than the market price, so the poor get food at affordable rates.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the role of cooperatives in ensuring food security.", a: "(1) In Tamil Nadu, about 94% of fair price shops are run by cooperatives.\n(2) Mother Dairy in Delhi sells milk and vegetables at controlled rates, and Amul in Gujarat brought the White Revolution.\n(3) The Academy of Development Science in Maharashtra helped set up grain banks in different regions.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What is meant by food security? Why is it needed, and what happens to it during a natural calamity?", a: "(1) Food security means availability, accessibility and affordability of food to all people at all times.\n(2) Availability is food production, imports and the stock in government granaries.\n(3) Accessibility means food is within every person's reach, and affordability means enough money to buy sufficient, safe and nutritious food.\n(4) In a natural calamity such as drought, production falls and prices rise, so the poor cannot buy food.\n(5) A long calamity may cause starvation and famine, as in the Bengal Famine of 1943, which killed about thirty lakh people.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Describe the main food programmes of the Government of India that aim to ensure food security.", a: "(1) Rationing began in the 1940s after the Bengal famine and was revived in the 1960s during food shortages.\n(2) ICDS (1975) and Food-for-Work (1977–78) were introduced in the 1970s.\n(3) The Revamped PDS (1992) targeted remote and backward blocks, and the Targeted PDS (1997) targeted the poor in all areas.\n(4) The Antyodaya Anna Yojana (2000) gave 25 kg (later raised to 35 kg) of grain to the poorest families at ₹2 per kg for wheat and ₹3 per kg for rice, and the Annapurna Scheme (2000) gave 10 kg of free grain to indigent senior citizens.\n(5) The National Food Security Act, 2013 covers 75% of the rural and 50% of the urban population as eligible households.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Kamla is a casual labourer in a town, and she holds a ration card. Last month, the shopkeeper told her that the grain stock was over, though the card entitled her to wheat at a price lower than the market. Later she saw the same shopkeeper selling rice in the open market.\n(i) What is the price at which ration shops sell grains to the poor called?\n(ii) Which problem of the PDS is shown here?\n(iii) Name the agency which procures grains for the buffer stock.", a: "(i) The issue price.\n(ii) Diversion of grain meant for ration shops to the open market by the dealer.\n(iii) The Food Corporation of India (FCI).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
